  env_cfg   cfg;
  bus_agent agt[];
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction

  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(env_cfg)::get(this, "", "cfg", cfg))
      `uvm_fatal("CFG", "env_cfg not found")
    agt = new[cfg.num_agents];
    foreach (agt[i]) begin
      // "agtN*" covers the agent and its driver / monitor / sequencer
      uvm_config_db#(agent_cfg)::set(this, $sformatf("agt%0d*", i), "cfg", cfg.agt_cfg[i]);
      agt[i] = bus_agent::type_id::create($sformatf("agt%0d", i), this);
    end
  endfunction
endclass
```

### Why this scales
- **Few, scoped lookups:** config_db cost grows with the number of entries and wildcard matches. One object per level keeps it small; avoid `set(null, "*", ...)`.
- **Type safety and defaults:** fields live in a class with defaults and constraints, so they can be randomized and printed.
- **Shared handle:** components hold the same object, so a run-time change (for example, a test flipping a field) is visible everywhere without a new `set`.
- **Test overrides:** derived tests override only `configure()`.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `set` must happen **before** the child's `build_phase` calls `get` (top-down build guarantees this if you set before `create`).
- Precedence: during build, a `set` from a higher component wins over a lower one; later sets win only at the same level.
- A mismatched type parameter between `set` and `get` (`int` vs `int unsigned`) silently fails; always check `get`'s return value.
- Debug with `+UVM_CONFIG_DB_TRACE` and `check_config_usage()` to find unused or misspelled entries.

### Follow-ups
- *Why not assign handles directly (`env.cfg = cfg`)?* It works and is fastest, but it couples the test to the env internals; config_db keeps components reusable stand-alone.
- *How to react to run-time changes?* `uvm_config_db#(T)::wait_modified(...)`, or poll a field of the shared config object.

linkedin-uvm-002:
**Key idea:** "Late" items are almost always a **phasing or handshake** problem, not a sequencer bug: the sequence starts in the wrong phase or without an objection, the driver waits for a clock edge *before* calling `get_next_item()`, or `item_done()` is called late so the sequencer cannot arbitrate the next item. Debug with phase/objection tracing and timestamps, then fix by (1) putting reset/config/traffic into the right run-time phases and (2) writing the driver so it fetches the next item in the same cycle the previous one completes.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant SEQ as sequence
  participant SQR as sequencer
  participant DRV as driver
  participant DUT as DUT
  DRV->>SQR: get_next_item (blocks until an item is ready)
  SEQ->>SQR: start_item / finish_item
  SQR-->>DRV: req
  DRV->>DUT: drive valid and data at this clock edge
  DUT-->>DRV: ready sampled at next edge
  DRV->>SQR: item_done
  SQR-->>SEQ: finish_item returns
  DRV->>SQR: get_next_item again in the same cycle
```

### Debug checklist
| Step | How |
| --- | --- |
| Which phase does each side live in? | `+UVM_PHASE_TRACE`; is the sequence in `main_phase` while the driver waits on reset in `run_phase`? |
| Does the phase end or stall early? | `+UVM_OBJECTION_TRACE`; missing `raise_objection` makes the phase end before items flow |
| Where is the time lost? | log `$time` at `start_item`, grant, `get_next_item` return, and `item_done`; compare with the waveform |
| Is the sequencer starved? | sequencer `UVM_HIGH` verbosity shows arbitration; check `lock`/`grab` and other sequences |
| Is the sequence blocked on `get_response()`? | the driver must `put_response()` / `item_done(rsp)` if the sequence waits for one |

### Code
